Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS — Enable content authors using the Contentstack CMS to see real-time preview of their content changes reflected in the application without requiring a full page reload or republish.

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is the single source that computes and freezes the content object for the entire app. It uses useMemo with [defaultContent] as the only dependency, making the content immutable after initial render. All 236+ consumers via useContent → useContentstackContext read from this frozen value, so no live preview update can ever reach them. The fix must go here — replacing the frozen memo with updatable state and wiring the Contentstack SDK's live preview callback to push changes into that state.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the useMemo block (lines 56-61) with useState initialized from defaultContent, and add a useEffect that subscribes to the Contentstack SDK's Stack.livePreview.onEntryChange callback to call the state setter with the updated content entry. Specifically: (1) Change `const context = useMemo(() => ({ content: defaultContent }), [defaultContent])` to `const [content, setContent] = useState<IContentstackContent>(defaultContent)`. (2) Add `useEffect(() => { Stack.livePreview.onEntryChange((entry) => { setContent((prev) => ({ ...prev, page: [entry] })); }); }, [])` — adjust the merge shape to match IContentstackContent based on which fields the live preview entry maps to. (3) Update the Provider value to `{ content }` instead of `context`. (4) Add `useEffect(() => { setContent(defaultContent) }, [defaultContent])` so full page navigations still update content. This is the minimal change: one frozen memo → one updatable state + one subscription.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- Given the app is opened from the Contentstack CMS preview panel, the visible page renders the current published content from the CMS on first load.
- Given an author edits content in the CMS but has not yet saved or published it during the preview session, the on-screen content remains unchanged until the author saves/publishes the edit.
- Given the author saves/publishes an edit in the CMS preview session, the new text appears on the visible screen while the same app session continues, without closing or restarting the preview.
- Given the author updates a card's title text in the CMS and saves/publishes it, the content card displayed on the page shows the updated title text.
- Given the author updates the sign-in dialog's heading text in the CMS and saves/publishes it, the sign-in dialog displayed to the user shows the updated heading text.
- Given a CMS-driven text value is updated and saved/published, every visible region of the page that displays that value shows the updated text and no region continues to show the stale text.
- Given content updates arrive during a live preview session, they appear on screen without any visible full page reload, browser navigation, or page flicker.

